# Appendix A — Program assembly maps

The main chapters explain why Literate is designed as it is and show the fragments
that realize each idea. This appendix records how those fragments are gathered
into complete generated files. These roots are essential to tangling the program,
but their ordering is mechanical rather than part of the main argument.

A root below can be read as a compact table of contents for one generated module.
Its child names point back to the chapters where the corresponding ideas are
explained. Readers can use this map to move between a complete file and the ideas
from which it is assembled.

## Assemble the browser Mermaid preview

The browser entry first acquires its local renderer and theme catalogue. It then
combines the state, policy, source tracking, rendering queue, scheduling, and
startup behavior explained in [Browser Mermaid preview](mermaid_preview.html).

```js : <<assemble the browser Mermaid preview.*>>= ./src/mermaid-preview.js $
import mermaid from 'mermaid';
import { getTheme, defaultThemeName, themeCss, mermaidTheme } from './themes';

<<Mermaid preview state>>
<<Mermaid preview configuration>>
<<Mermaid preview source tracking>>
<<Mermaid preview rendering>>
<<Mermaid preview scheduling>>
<<Mermaid preview startup>>
```

## Assemble the shared annotation model

The shared annotation module joins its renderer-neutral contracts, restricted
body parser, stable identities, safe links, and complete collection pass. Their
purpose and constraints are developed in
[Shared publication annotations](publication-annotations.html).

```ts : <<assemble the shared annotation model.*>>= ./src/publication-annotations.ts $
<<annotation contracts>>
<<annotation body parsing>>
<<annotation identities and hrefs>>
<<annotation collection>>
```

## Assemble projected language services

Language intelligence begins with an in-memory representation of the source files
that fragment expansion would produce. The projection module combines its public
model, graph validation, literal mappings, expansion, templates, and coordinate
helpers as explained in [Mapped language sources](language-projection.html).

```ts : <<assemble projected language sources.*>>= ./src/language-projection.ts $
<<projection contracts>>
<<projection entry>>
<<projection dependency validation>>
<<projection literal segments>>
<<projection expansion>>
<<projection emission>>
<<projection templates>>
<<projection position helpers>>
```

